<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vedas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vedas are the oldest scriptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, composed in Vedic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about 1500 BCE and transmitted orally for centuries. There are four, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaveda">Samaveda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a>, each with hymns, ritual texts and later philosophical works including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a>. They remain the foundation of Hindu ritual and learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vedas.jpg" alt="Vedas" width="320" height="132" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1500-1200_BCE_Rigveda,_manuscript_page_sample_i,_Mandala_1,_Hymn_1_(Sukta_1),_Adhyaya_1,_lines_1.1.1_to_1.1.9,_Sanskrit,_Devanagari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
